{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"2d9833d0c4d8cf01","type":"group","x":-1115,"y":6220,"width":300,"height":745,"label":"Untitled group"},
		{"id":"95c2b48afe743c04","type":"group","x":-1100,"y":7060,"width":285,"height":500,"label":"Untitled group"},
		{"id":"bf1f8f4f0d527b8e","type":"group","x":880,"y":5520,"width":290,"height":440,"label":"structures"},
		{"id":"72a52ca126921684","type":"file","file":"Do Not Publish/Technology, Purpose & Agency.md","x":-780,"y":-1160,"width":740,"height":8720},
		{"id":"04c1f8ff81d86eb1","type":"text","text":"For example, The plow... permanent settlements... ownership of land... new relationships to animals, new relationship to land, gender etc, change in values...\n\nThe development of [land surveying and cartography](https://www.radicalxchange.org/media/blog/towards-decentralized-civic-infrastructure/) in 17th century Europe transformed land into bounded commodities and enabled the concept of state jurisdiction through geographical territory. Widespread literacy enabled by the printing press restructured the one-to-many relationship between knowledge producers and consumers....\n\nThe internet is a teletransportation technology... collapses space, creates new kinds of digital spaces...","x":-1435,"y":974,"width":615,"height":329},
		{"id":"4bfea786921afbb3","type":"text","text":"In essence, technology amplifies our capabilities to act on the world, while simultaneously weaving individual humans into an interconnected organism.","x":-1070,"y":527,"width":250,"height":200},
		{"id":"467a1b8ee7c1d39d","type":"text","text":"Technologies are shaped by the interplay of societal superstructures, social structures, and infrastructures. For example, the development of the printing press was influenced by the Renaissance's emphasis on knowledge-sharing (superstructure), enabled by the the rise of affluent merchant classes and relatively stable centralized governments (social structure), while building upon prior innovations like movable type and the printing mechanism itself (infrastructure).","x":-1560,"y":527,"width":380,"height":393},
		{"id":"892bb426555a1c57","type":"text","text":"Talk more about how the plow changed belief systems, not just animal domestication > belief in domination. Did the change in beliefs come first ie domination over a group of humans?","x":-1070,"y":734,"width":250,"height":240,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3e54c695bdc17bf2","type":"text","text":"### Technology & Influence\n> *Technologies always create the potential for new forms of behavior, values, and thought, even when the technology is not explicitly made to do so.* [Technology is Not Values Neutral: Ending the Reign of Nihilistic Design](https://consilienceproject.org/technology-is-not-values-neutral)\n\nTechnologies are shaped by the interplay of societal superstructures, social structures, and infrastructures. For example, the development of the printing press was influenced by the Renaissance's emphasis on knowledge-sharing (superstructure), enabled by the the rise of affluent merchant classes and relatively stable centralized governments (social structure), while building upon prior innovations like movable type and the printing mechanism itself (infrastructure).\n\nHowever, technologies are not merely passive products of civilization - they actively reconfigure the very contexts that gave rise to them. Technologies reshape how we perceive and interact with the world by introducing new constraints and affordances that alter the boundaries of time, space, relationships, and values. \n\n- **Time**: Technologies affect our perceptions of what is [likely to be true in the future](https://stark.mirror.xyz/n2UpRqwdf7yjuiPKVICPpGoUNeDhlWxGqjulrlpyYi0), as well as what was true in the past.\n- **Space**: Technologies can create and transcend spatial constraints, reshaping the experience and politics of geography, distance, and place.\n- **Relationships**: Technologies mediate the types and qualities of relationships possible between agents.\n- **Values**: Technologies open up new modes of being that cultivate revisioned value systems.\n\nIn this way, technologies are imbued with the priorities and belief systems of their creators, which then propagate through societies in complex, often unpredictable ways. This is particularly true of information technologies, which directly shape how we process, share, and construct meaning. ","x":-1891,"y":-470,"width":765,"height":790},
		{"id":"64716ac5b958a90a","type":"text","text":"Rock is simple tech\nwashing machine is complicated tech\nwhen you add networks it becomes complex","x":0,"y":-500,"width":250,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"624823edc73c0b5d","type":"text","text":"How is this related to cybernetics as described above? Social structure is the control structure. Where does superstructure come in?","x":-1095,"y":6235,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"7d0b88d4c163dedf","type":"text","text":"How does this happen?","x":-1095,"y":6460,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"f5dd15ff490e81bb","type":"text","text":"Talk about info control theory here?","x":-1095,"y":6545,"width":250,"height":90,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"fed958db53d75069","type":"text","text":"Break this down into sub systems: gathering info, filtering info etc? How is this related to the structures?","x":-1095,"y":6660,"width":250,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"1f8e73bf2bee2bce","type":"text","text":"Why is this important? How does it help us meet our shared needs?","x":-1095,"y":6840,"width":250,"height":100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"9962d4dfe97c5624","type":"text","text":"Tech helps us adapt to our environment, but it also adapts our environment and us at the same time","x":-1080,"y":7085,"width":250,"height":140,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ac7763dd108c8bdc","type":"text","text":"Use wheat as an example: domesticated us","x":-1080,"y":7259,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"188644c32f1d3dc2","type":"text","text":"To the extent that tech helps us adapt to the ecosystem and it to us in order to thrive, tech adapts us and itself thrives","x":-1080,"y":7343,"width":250,"height":161,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5d74e39e7df96732","type":"text","text":"For example, one important “cast” about gold is that its supply will remain predictable in the future. We could express this precisely as “between X and Y kilograms of gold will enter the market each year for the next 20 years”.\n\nOr, consider a loan agreement. The cast here might be something like “If Alice does not pay Bob back $100 before July 1, then the legal institutions of my jurisdiction will use increasingly severe threats and actions to force her to pay Bob”.\n\nIn the case of a digital asset on Ethereum, one cast might be “this asset can only be transferred if a transaction is signed using the private key that corresponds to the public key X”.","x":-2134,"y":374,"width":470,"height":432,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"7f8b7a0db785948a","type":"text","text":"Maybe here we lightly introduce cybernetic theories via ANT?","x":-2384,"y":401,"width":250,"height":126,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"fad59352de1bf833","type":"text","text":"The nature of relationships between humans, tech and ecosystems","x":-2424,"y":560,"width":250,"height":107,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"ed3869af6fad5a4a","type":"text","text":"How does tech help us build civilization? It is the stuff that civilization is made outof. Building blocks of civilization.","x":-1820,"y":1154,"width":250,"height":150,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"7e4c6e320a994cd0","type":"text","text":"These technologies encompass a wide range of tools and systems, from the invention of writing and the printed word to the rise of markets, digital communication networks and artificial intelligence. ","x":-2450,"y":1754,"width":400,"height":200},
		{"id":"3e33ac87e6039f45","type":"text","text":"### What is [[1.3.1 Superstructure]]?\nThe superstructure encompasses a society's beliefs, values, and worldviews, shaping and being shaped by how its members perceive and interact with the world. It includes the symbolic and ideational aspects of a culture, such as:\n- **Religion**: Belief systems, rituals, and religious institutions that provide meaning and guidance for human existence.\n- **Art and Literature**: Cultural expressions and artistic practices that reflect and shape a society's values and narratives.\n- **Ideology**: Overarching worldviews, values, and belief systems that justify and support the existing social order.\n\nThe way our perception of ourselves is formed, as well as how we interpret information, is mediated by superstructure. Rather than \"seeing is believing,\" our beliefs fundamentally influence what we perceive.\n### What is [[1.3.2 Social Structure]]?\nSocial structure refers to the organized set of institutions, norms, and relationships that shape and regulate the behavior and interactions of individuals within a society. It includes:\n- **Domestic Economy**: Family organization and household economic activities.\n- **Political Economy**: Patterns of authority, laws, and governance within a society, including the organization of power and the distribution of resources.\n\nMany of our interactions with each other are mediated by social structure. For example, the family unit acts as a core social structure, governing dynamics like gender roles, distribution of labor, and norms around intimate relationships.\n### What is [[1.3.3 Infrastructure]]?\nInfrastructure refers to the integrated ecologies of technologies that provision society's essential needs, including:\n- **Means of Production**: Physical and technological means necessary for producing goods and services (e.g., factories, machinery, tools).\n- **Technological Innovations**: Advancements that affect productivity, efficiency, and the capacity to harness and utilize natural resources.\n\nMost of our interactions with the world are mediated by infrastructure. You might be sitting in your house right now. In that case, your house is a technological infrastructure by which you're currently interacting with the planetary ecosystem.","x":-2579,"y":974,"width":559,"height":660},
		{"id":"dfd59ac641c6c85a","type":"text","text":"Ok, so in the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Confederacy example, before the confederacy each nation was a metasystem, with their own subsystems, but the creation of the confederacy was a metasystem transition in which a new larger metasystem emerged which was the confederacy and the nations became subsystems within this larger metasystem","x":-2940,"y":745,"width":473,"height":189},
		{"id":"f4f3d117f81fd062","type":"text","text":"![[Info-Energy-Materials.png]]","x":-2745,"y":1754,"width":250,"height":260},
		{"id":"80c57f09ced3a787","type":"text","text":"Institutions are systems of rules, norms, and practices that structure human behavior and interactions. While we may not typically think of institutions as technologies, they can be understood as such through the lens of the MEI model. By structuring human behavior and interactions in specific ways, institutions shape how we allocate resources, make decisions, and create meaning in our lives. They are technologies for coordinating social action and achieving collective goals.\n","x":-1800,"y":3630,"width":400,"height":350},
		{"id":"cc44a4e0b833c53a","type":"text","text":"Language offers another fascinating example of an institutional technology. Its moral purpose is profound: to enable communication, foster understanding, and preserve knowledge across time and space. The protocols of language are intricate and evolving, encompassing grammar, syntax, vocabulary, and unwritten rules of conversation. As for actants, they include every speaker and listener, writer and reader, as well as the physical and digital media through which language flows. When we converse, we're not just exchanging words—we're participating in an ancient and ever-evolving institution.\n\n\n\nIn each of these examples—marriage, language, and internet protocols—we see how the interplay between moral purpose, protocols, and actants creates a system that's far more than the sum of its parts. By examining the moral purposes, protocols, and actants that compose an institution, we can gain insights into their functions, limitations, and potential for change.","x":-1800,"y":4060,"width":520,"height":580},
		{"id":"63da9181756f9ebd","type":"text","text":"Information technologies are particularly important because they directly shape how we process, share, and make sense of the world. The information technology [underpinning a society](https://www.radicalxchange.org/media/blog/towards-decentralized-civic-infrastructure/) profoundly impacts its structure and outcomes. For example, the development of land surveying and cartography in 17th century Europe transformed land into bounded commodities and enabled the concept of state jurisdiction through geographical territory. ","x":-2840,"y":2324,"width":340,"height":380},
		{"id":"a38be161c0b102ee","type":"text","text":"Metasystems occur when environmental conditions favor an adaptive pressure for increased biological or technological information processing capability (Smith and Szathmáry, 1995). Under these conditions functional synergies between cooperative agents that were previously impossible (in this context geographically separated human groups) can produce the emergence of higher complexity and control organization (Corning, 2002, 2014). The coordination of higher controls begins by the cooperative/inclusive utilization of emergent information processing capabilities in a collective information medium, which allows for qualitatively new interaction\npotentiality (Heylighen, 2006). Control innovation utilizing a new information medium can then allow for higher integration and a new level of complex organization (Heylighen, 1995). Throughout human evolution control innovation has occurred on three emergent information mediums: language, writing, and the printing press\n(Last, 2014), which have changed the qualitative nature of human interaction potential (bands, kingdoms, nations, etc.). ","x":-2600,"y":3394,"width":560,"height":460},
		{"id":"a56aa802ba4759f3","type":"text","text":"Materials as a source of hardness, also energy: if it takes a lot of energy to travel across the network that creates different constraints","x":-2575,"y":2715,"width":250,"height":199},
		{"id":"d0356205031b003b","type":"text","text":"new tech creates new constraints and affordances on how we can act on the world, and each other. This essentially changes the shape of reality. This can apply to materials: making new kinds of materials can shape what our civilization can do, for example, steel. Also energy: if it takes a lot of energy to travel across the network that creates different constraints, for example the effects to democracy of having to travel by horse across the country to receive a message vs immediate. This is also  an example of how information creates contraints: information effects from internet networks vs paper-based comms like newspaper. Information technologies are particularly important because they directly shape how we process, share, and make sense of the world. \n","x":-2450,"y":2324,"width":400,"height":370},
		{"id":"c4aa4448c460f6f2","type":"text","text":"In this way, technologies are never value-neutral but imbued with the priorities and belief systems of their creators, which then propagate through societies in complex, often unpredictable ways.","x":-2360,"y":3040,"width":250,"height":248},
		{"id":"8afe2418bf7e6059","type":"text","text":"**Technologies determine how we organize our society.**","x":-2840,"y":2715,"width":250,"height":94},
		{"id":"87b9a21017475353","type":"text","text":"In other words, nation-states are insufficient structures to deal  with global problems which threaten the future of human stability and also prevent the possible achievement of a future peaceful human-world symbiosis, as originally envisioned by the founders of enlightenment (e.g. Kant, 1784).\n\nthe list of global problems that appear endemic and make our system increasingly fragile are all challenges too great for any one nation-state: ecological footprint, CO2 emissions, forest areas, fresh water resources...\n\nFurthermore, there is little hope that these issues can be solved within our existing structures, as nation-states have become increasingly susceptible to international corporate influence, which erodes democracy and directs control attention away from people and ecology, and toward corporate profit only\n\nThese emergent problems are not problems because we lack the intelligence to acknowledge and address them. Instead, these problems are problems because we lack the effective mechanisms for fostering and maintaining new forms of distributed intelligence, which can maximize new governance potential, and create a more inclusive world.\n[[Information-energy metasystem model]]","x":-2600,"y":3954,"width":560,"height":645},
		{"id":"6dcaa9dc7623b2c0","type":"text","text":"Now more than ever, we are building exponential technologies that have the potential to transform our world in profound ways. However, the driving force behind technological development is often money, rather than ethics or the greater good. If we don't find a way to reconcile our technological abilities with social and natural capital, we risk nothing less than the collapse of our civilization.","x":-2920,"y":4019,"width":250,"height":480},
		{"id":"d39100e6b6aaf6cf","type":"text","text":"The way we design, develop, and deploy technologies can either reinforce existing power structures and inequalities or create opportunities for more equitable and sustainable futures. By recognizing the inherent value-laden nature of technology, we can work towards creating technologies that align with our desired outcomes and contribute to the well-being of both humans and the planet.","x":-2870,"y":4629,"width":400,"height":270},
		{"id":"0bd426d6cbb332fe","type":"text","text":"(Interestingly, these three domains – nuclear/energy, AI/information, and biotech/materials – mirror the fundamental components of technology as the structuring of energy, information, and materials. This symmetry underscores the urgency of governing these powerful tools to align with human ethics and ecological boundaries.)","x":-2400,"y":4632,"width":360,"height":260},
		{"id":"5dc6ac2d77d17757","type":"text","text":"As our industrial civilization faces a confluence of existential crises - from catastrophic climate change to resource depletion, biodiversity loss, and technological disruption - it has become evident that we must transition to a new civilizational model to ensure a thriving future for humanity and our planetary home.","x":-2560,"y":5654,"width":449,"height":230},
		{"id":"3f0219f6f66fffc6","type":"text","text":"## Why do we need regeneration?\nSolving the critical problems humans are facing feels like a game of whack-a-mole:\n1. In the rush to implement green energy, new landscapes are being destroyed to source rare minerals, while fossil fuel use grows at an increasingly faster rate;\n2. Digital technologies, which some believed would one day  \"democratize everything\", are causing our democracies to collapse by driving misinformation and polarization;\n3. In the name of progress, we are experimenting with artificial intelligence and biotechnology, which exponentially increase civilizational risks. \n\nWhy is it that every time we cut off the head of the dragon, three more grow in its place? Because we keep treating the symptoms without facing the real disease: a large-scale breakdown in coordination across human society known as the [[Metacrisis]]. This breakdown is fundamentally a manifestation of widespread failures to interact holistically within a complex system.\n\nThe [Mechanistic Mindset](https://diome.xyz/4+%F0%9F%8C%B0+Seeds/Mechanistic+Mindset), which has dominated much of modern thought, is characterized by the belief that complex systems can be understood and managed by dissecting them into their individual components, similar to how a machine operates. This mindset leads to human systems and ways of knowing that neglect the emergent properties and non-linear interactions that are crucial in complex systems. The unintended consequences of this inability to coordinate within complex systems are known in economics as [Externalities](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Externalities).\n\nOur current civilization is built on credit, borrowing from the bank of natural and social capital we need to sustain life in the long term. The longer we live outside of our means, the higher the risk of sliding towards catastrophic and dystopic futures. If we don't treat the root cause by repurposing our systems and institutions to support the flourishing of life on earth, no amount of solar panels will change the game.","x":-2560,"y":5968,"width":541,"height":980},
		{"id":"9dec4c6548b38a95","type":"text","text":"Here something more like: we're already seeing the 3 attractors emerging & give examples of each","x":-2519,"y":7020,"width":250,"height":140,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"fd011e467e9b44ab","type":"text","text":"Put the 3 long explanations into their own notes and just have a summary here in bullet points","x":-2269,"y":7020,"width":250,"height":125,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3103af480e123d43","type":"text","text":"1. Superstructure: The beliefs, values, and worldviews, shaping and being shaped by how humans perceive and interact with the world. \n\t- Acts as the \"programming\" of the cybernetic system\n\t- Provides goals and evaluation criteria for the system\n\t- Influences how information is interpreted and processed\n\t- Shapes values, beliefs and priorities about material use, energy consumption, network organization, and resource allocation\n\t- Determines the perceived importance of information, energy, materials and networks.\n\n2. Social Structure: The organized set of institutions, norms, and relationships that shape and regulate the behavior and interactions of individuals.\n\t- Serves as the \"processing unit\" of the system\n\t- Facilitates information flow and decision-making\n\t- Implements responses based on processed information\n\t- Organizes the collection, distribution, and use of information, materials and energy\n\t- Manages networks for social, economic, and political purposes\n\t- Mediates conflicts over resources\n\n3. Infrastructure (Physical Systems, Technology): Infrastructure refers to the integrated ecologies of technologies that provision society's essential needs.\n\t- Functions as the \"sensors\" and \"actuators\" of the system\n\t- Gathers information from the environment\n\t- Implements changes in the physical world\n\t- Comprises the physical manifestation of material use and energy systems\n\t- Includes the tangible aspects of networks (roads, communication systems, etc.)","x":-2019,"y":5614,"width":550,"height":750},
		{"id":"87b9dfb670c0ee43","type":"text","text":"Disruptions in ecosystems, such as climate change or the depletion of essential resources, can put pressure on human societies to adapt their technologies, economies, and lifestyles, leading to cascading effects across all other elements. ","x":-2450,"y":1984,"width":400,"height":170},
		{"id":"5ab0b8aef12a5103","type":"text","text":"It arises from the interplay between a group's worldviews, social institutions, and technological infrastructures. ","x":-2360,"y":-366,"width":250,"height":200},
		{"id":"5b7c037b054f38f5","type":"text","text":"==(This is not a new aspect of the globalized system,)== Even a seemingly simple object like a woven basket reveals similar complexity: it embodies information passed down through generations via language, consists of materials like wood that are part of vast ecological networks, requires human energy sustained by intricate food systems, and relies on social structures that allow for specialization. \n","x":-1240,"y":4960,"width":250,"height":120},
		{"id":"5c87847f35a6f9db","type":"text","text":"==(it's always been the case, just on a smaller scale: language is a technology, food is energy, families are networks, everything in our ecosystem can be a material depending on our relationship to it, for example a rock. What makes human special: tech? cooperation? animals do both. Being human is about massively extending networks of cooperation and influence through technology, from language to money to the internet. Last line ties this into the next bit about civilization, maybe something like, to really understand the effects of tech we have to zoom out and look at the big picture?)==","x":-1420,"y":5140,"width":430,"height":340},
		{"id":"270264bfe38ef368","type":"text","text":"Like any network, institutions can absorb local disruptions without overall failure. At the same time, small changes can sometimes propagate rapidly through the network, leading to large-scale shifts. ","x":-1140,"y":4100,"width":310,"height":200},
		{"id":"f7dd0697449a82ba","type":"text","text":"==(we mentioned internet protocols, but this web of relationships extends far beyond digital networks.  we mentioned a computer: stuff made into components, energy that powers it, code, money, institutions that make all the stuff work etc, electric grids, internet, supply chain)==","x":-1205,"y":4560,"width":250,"height":300},
		{"id":"445ded4acb6b722b","type":"text","text":"Also: 1. you are acting through the rock on something else, 2. you are acting directly on the rock","x":-40,"y":1040,"width":250,"height":131,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"8aec0af665c1aae4","type":"text","text":"Most of our interactions with planetary ecosystems are mediated by infrastrcuture. For example, you might be sitting in your house right now. In that case, your house is a technological infrastructure by which you're currently interacting with the planetary ecosystem.","x":-3320,"y":1634,"width":380,"height":200},
		{"id":"d79ffde99b044977","type":"text","text":"## What is it?\n\n## Why is it important?\n\n## How does it need to change?\n\n## Questions","x":14112,"y":-414,"width":300,"height":296},
		{"id":"8af578f7bb60ba25","type":"text","text":"## Rediscovering Sacredness\nThere is a growing interest in spirituality which seeks to find meaning and value beyond the limitations of both traditional religion and modern secularism. This shift is characterized by a reconnection with the greater whole, a sense of coming home to place, and a view of technology as a sacred process rather than a purely utilitarian tool. Rediscovering sacredness is essential  because it helps shift our collective values from purely materialistic to one that recognizes the intrinsic value of nature, community, and the human experience.\n- Sacred ideas\n\t- [Metamodern spirituality](https://m.youtube.com/watch?v=bSSb4IPNMGI) \n\t- [First principles & first values](https://www.amazon.com/First-Principles-Values-Propositions-Cosmoerotic/dp/B0CS85WYVX#customerReviews)\n\t- [Conscious evolution](https://youtube.com/watch?v=I4XtGTWx050&si=NZUyO1lC1DLm0yot)\n\t- [Sacred Design Lab](https://sacred.design/)\n\t- [Romance of reality](https://www.amazon.co.uk/Romance-Reality-Organizes-Consciousness-Complexity-ebook/dp/B09GW3G45J)\n\t- Awakening from the meaning crisis\n- Sacred places\n\t- [Sacred Civics - Building Seven Generation Cities](https://www.taylorfrancis.com/books/oa-edit/10.4324/9781003199816/sacred-civics-jayne-engle-julian-agyeman-tanya-chung-tiam-fook)\n\t- [Spaces for the Sacred](https://books.google.com/books/about/Spaces_for_the_Sacred.html?hl=es&id=EqvOBAAACAAJ)\n- Sacred technologies\n\t- [WHAT IF TECHNOLOGY WERE A PRAYER?](https://sacred.display.cz/prayer)\n- Sacred experiences\n\t- [Floating](https://www.feelslikefloating.com/about)\n\t- Singing with Nightingales\n\t- [Nearness](https://www.nearness.coop/)\n## Redefining Interactions\nThe metaphor of mycelium, the vast underground fungal network, symbolizes the interconnectedness of all beings. In this understanding, the self is not seen as a fixed, isolated entity but rather as a fluid and co-created concept that emerges through interactions with others and the environment. Just as mycelium facilitates nutrient exchange in ecosystems, our social interactions support collective growth and well-being. As a result, there is a growing emphasis on fostering meaningful connections and building deeper, more empathetic relationships with others.\n- [Identity](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Identity)\n- Interaction practices\n\t- Non-Violent Communication (NVC)\n\t- Authentic Relating\n\t- Warm Data Lab\n## Rebuilding Moral Ecosystems\n[Moral ecosystems](https://subpixel.space/entries/moral-ecosystems?utm_source=tobyshorin&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=moral-ecosystems-my-big-idea) refer to networks of individuals and communities that are united by shared moral beliefs, values, and practices, as well as the institutions that support and develop these morals. Historically, religious institutions served as the primary moral ecosystems, providing a framework for ethical behavior and a sense of shared purpose. As religious institutions have declined in influence, brands and consumer lifestyles have increasingly taken on this role. Now, we are witnessing the emergence of more distributed moral ecosystems that are not tied to traditional institutions or brands, but sustained by online networks and communities. How might new moral ecosystems develop that incorporate emerging ideas of sacredness with cutting-edge infrastructure to transform society?\n## Developing Wisdom\nWe need to reimagine our learning systems to be life-long, autonomous, self-directed, contextual, personalized and to transcend traditional educational settings.\n- [Education is the Metacrisis](https://systems-souls-society.com/education-is-the-metacrisis/)\n- [Democratic Education](https://eudec.org/democratic-education/what-is-democratic-education/)\n- [City As A School](https://www.cityasaschool.com/)\n- [Kernel Learning Community](https://www.kernel.community/en/)\n- AI learning copilots\n- [Transforming Education to Prevent Catastrophe](https://www.su.org/feedback-loop/transforming-education-to-prevent-catastrophe)\n- [Swedish Popular Education](https://escapingflatland.substack.com/p/popular-education-in-sweden-much)\n- Close observations of nature and complex systems guide knowledge and skill creation\n- Embodied knowledge","x":13462,"y":-414,"width":580,"height":591},
		{"id":"81cd0937bcb80a45","type":"text","text":"## Distributed Power Structures\nDistributed power structures follow 3 key principles: Ownership, Subsidiarity, Interdependence, Diversity & Emergence. (1) Ownership means all affected stakeholders have access to meaningful participation. (2) Subsidiarity means following a holonic structure, where decision-making groups are nested from the small & local to the global scale, and decisions are made at the lowest possible level. (3) Interdependence means these groups form a meshwork, erasing artificial separations between groups, organizations or geographies. (4) Diversity means not just involving multiple voices, but allowing for [multiple choices, opportunities, and encounters](https://provocations.darkmatterlabs.org/re-permissioning-the-city-unlocking-cities-growing-underutilised-spatial-assets-for-an-emergent-1550997714a4). (5) Emergence is like \"birds in a flock that, over time, [builds coherence](https://provocations.darkmatterlabs.org/re-permissioning-the-city-unlocking-cities-growing-underutilised-spatial-assets-for-an-emergent-1550997714a4) and order by simply responding to the movements of their neighbours.\"\n- [Cooperatives & Community Exits](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Cooperatives+%26+Community+Exits)\n- DAOs\n- [Mondragon](https://www.mondragon-corporation.com/)\n- [Zebras Unite](https://zebrasunite.coop/)\n- [DisCO](https://manifesto.disco.coop/)\n- [Nested Bioregionalism & Regenerative Civilization](https://www.recommon.land/blog/nested-bioregionalism)\n- [Self-Governance of The Commons](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Self-Governance+of+The+Commons)\n- Neighborhood Democracy\n- [RePermissioning the City](https://diome.xyz/5+%F0%9F%8C%BC+Flowers/RePermissioning+the+City)\n- [Participatory City](https://diome.xyz/5+%F0%9F%8C%BC+Flowers/Participatory+City)\n- [Participation At Scale Can Repair The Public Square](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Participation+At+Scale+Can+Repair+The+Public+Square)\n- [CIVIC SQUARE (2020-2030)](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/CIVIC+SQUARE+(2020-2030))\n- [Community Tech](https://www.communitytech.network/blog-community-tech/community-tech-discovery-fund-our-reflections)\n- [Neighborhood organizing](https://sonec.org/sample-page)\n- Communal land ownership & stewardship\n- Solar communities\n## Collective Intelligence\nCollective intelligence refers to the capacity of groups, communities, or entire societies to pool knowledge, wisdom, and expertise in order to make decisions, solve problems, and govern more effectively. It leverages diversity of thought, interdisciplinary approaches, and participatory mechanisms to enhance the quality and inclusiveness of governance.\n### Metagovernance Processes\n> _We tend to overemphasize short and shallow feedback cycles while ignoring the long and deep ones. Instead, we should increase our capacity to make fundamental and structural changes, while being less sensitive to short-term trends and pressures._ [Hanzi Freinacht](https://medium.com/@hanzifreinacht/3-design-principles-for-protopian-governance-bc2bfa7faa9a)\n- [Governing in Nonlinear Time](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Governing+in+Nonlinear+Time)\n### Knowledge Commons\nKnowledge commons refer to shared resources and information systems where knowledge is created, curated, and accessed collectively by the community. These commons are built on principles of open access, collaboration, and the free exchange of information, aiming to democratize knowledge and foster innovation through collective effort.\n- Wikipedia\n- Libraries\n- DeSci\n- [A New Vision of Artificial Intelligence for the People](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/A+New+Vision+of+Artificial+Intelligence+for+the+People)\n- Open Source\n    - [Open Ecology](https://www.opensourceecology.org/)\n    - [WikiHouse](https://www.wikihouse.cc/)\n### Wisdom Technologies\nWisdom Technologies refer to digital tools and platforms designed to enhance collective intelligence, facilitate deep understanding, and foster meaningful connections within communities. These technologies are not just about processing information efficiently but also about enriching human experience, wisdom, and understanding.\n- [Nature-Inspired Interfaces](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Nature-Inspired+Interfaces)\n- [Rewilding Social Digital Spaces](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Rewilding+Social+Digital+Spaces)\n- Digital Public Spaces\n    - [New_ Public](https://newpublic.org/)\n    - [Open Future](https://openfuture.eu/)\n- [Communal Knowledge Tools](https://diome.xyz/4+%F0%9F%8C%B0+Seeds/Communal+Knowledge+Tools)\n    - [WeCo](https://weco.io/)\n    - [Obsidian](https://obsidian.md/)\n- Protocols & Hyperstructures\n- [Data Ownership & Governance](https://diome.xyz/Data+Ownership+%26+Governance)\n- [Data Neutrality & Interoperability](https://diome.xyz/Data+Neutrality+%26+Interoperability)\n- [Indigenous AI](https://www.indigenous-ai.net/)\n- The Craft Internet\n    - [The Internet as a Lota](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/The+Internet+as+a+Lota)\n    - [Evolutionary Craft](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Evolutionary+Craft)\n    - [Folk Interfaces](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Folk+Interfaces)\n### Sensemaking Systems\nSensemaking spaces are environments designed to facilitate the collective interpretation and understanding of complex information, fostering a deeper comprehension of the world and our place within it.\n\n![CollectiveSenseMakingDemocracy.jpg](https://publish-01.obsidian.md/access/c2d4cd262bc1ce94a1c36832673bfb55/Images/CollectiveSenseMakingDemocracy.jpg)\n- Citizens Assemblies\n- Indigenous governance\n- [Democracy Next](https://www.demnext.org/)\n- [3D Wise Democracy](https://www.wd-pl.com/)\n- [Open Civics](https://opencivics.co/)\n- [Third Spaces](https://thesfcommons.substack.com/p/a-manifesto-the-next-era-of-third)\n- [Decidim](https://decidim.org/)\n- [Polis](https://pol.is/home)\n- [The Ecological Intelligence Agency](https://diome.xyz/5+%F0%9F%8C%BC+Flowers/The+Ecological+Intelligence+Agency)\n- [Taiwan Model](https://global.espreso.tv/how-to-free-the-future-of-ai-taiwan-model-can-help)\n- [Liquid Democracy](https://diome.xyz/4+%F0%9F%8C%B0+Seeds/Liquid+Democracy)\n- [Citizens' Assemblies](https://diome.xyz/4+%F0%9F%8C%B0+Seeds/Citizens'+Assemblies)\n- [Quadratic Voting](https://diome.xyz/4+%F0%9F%8C%B0+Seeds/Quadratic+Voting)\n- [the next era of third spaces as crucibles for meaning-making](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/the+next+era+of+third+spaces+as+crucibles+for+meaning-making)\n- Relationship-Based Journalism\n    - [What Could Happen If We Give Up Saving Journalism-](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/What+Could+Happen+If+We+Give+Up+Saving+Journalism-)\n    - Journalism org in Venezuela -\n- Bioregional Learning Centers\n    - [History of the Ideas Underlying the Balaton Group](https://donellameadows.org/archives/history-of-the-ideas-underlying-the-balaton-group/)\n    - [Design School For Regenerating the Earth](https://design-school-for-regenerating-earth.mn.co/)\n- Imagination Infrastructure\n- [Dynamicland](https://dynamicland.org/)\n### Money as a decision-making tool\n- Community currencies\n- Mutual credit\n- [Grassroots Economics](https://diome.xyz/grassrootseconomics.org/)\n- [Sardex- a Local Currency for a Circular Economy- (ITA)](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Sardex-+a+Local+Currency+for+a+Circular+Economy-+(ITA))\n- [Plural Money- A New Currency Design](https://diome.xyz/7+%F0%9F%8D%82+Compost/Plural+Money-+A+New+Currency+Design)\n- [Circles UBI](https://www.circlesubi.id/)\n- [Index Wallets](https://index.option.app/)\n- Can new currencies or economic systems incentivize us to make more of things we want, like trees?\n- Credits: carbon, biodiversity and beyond\n### Understanding Impact\nMeasures and internalizes [Externalities](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Externalities), so that every harm done is visible to it.\n- [OpenSC](https://opensc.org/)\n- [Sunflower EcoTech](https://sunflowereco.tech/)\n### Funding, Investment & Profit Structures\n[Regenerative Investment](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Regenerative+Investment) aims to grow the capacity of the system rather than extract\n- Common (public) goods and beyond\n- [Regenerative Business](https://diome.xyz/2+%F0%9F%8C%BF+Leaves/Regenerative+Business)\n- NounsDAO","x":13462,"y":236,"width":490,"height":1210},
		{"id":"ffb0e4fcc36919cb","type":"text","text":"## Win-Win Ecosystems\n[[Accumulation, Extraction & Abstraction]] lead to rivalrous systems, where the success of one participant comes at the expense of others. Consider the example of a harvest: if I hoard half of the yield, it means there is less available for others, and more food is needed overall to feed everyone. This scarcity mindset leads to competition, with each participant striving to accumulate the most resources for themselves.\n\nIn contrast, an anti-rivalrous system, such as commons-based agriculture, aligns incentives and promotes collaboration. In this model, the more each participant invests in the health and productivity of the shared agricultural system, the greater the total yield, and the more everyone benefits. The success of one contributes to the success of all.\n\nWhen building technology, this principle translates into creating open, shared core infrastructure that serves as a foundation for further innovation. Ideally, this core infrastructure is built on an open model or owned by no one, existing as an open protocol. This creates a standardized, accessible base layer upon which many people can build. By pushing power to the ends—allowing a diverse range of actors to develop on top of this foundation—the system enables competition and innovation while maintaining a stable, reliable core.\n\n![[Win-Win Tech Layers.png]]\n\n- [Decentralization in all the things](https://ffdweb.org/digest/decentralization-in-all-the-things/)\n- [We Need To Rewild The Internet](https://www.noemamag.com/we-need-to-rewild-the-internet/)\n## Regenerative Production\n### Complexification\nOur world is becoming increasingly complicated, decreasing complexity. Complex systems have are self-regulating and interconnected, where the outputs of one process become the inputs of another. If a computer breaks, it cannot repair itself; whereas if we cut ourselves, our body naturally heals. This self-healing property is a kind of automation arising from the inter-entanglement of complex systems.\n\nComplicated systems are inherently fragile, while complexity is highly resilient. By embracing complexity, we can create regenerative production systems that are resilient, adaptable, and self-sustaining. Permaculture systems, for instance, make use of complexity by designing interconnected and mutually supportive relationships between different elements of the system. \n\n- Agroforestry\n\t- [Terra Genesis](https://terra-genesis.com/)\n- Dumb cities\n\t- [The case for ... making low-tech 'dumb' cities instead of 'smart' ones](https://www.theguardian.com/cities/2020/jan/15/the-case-for-making-low-tech-dumb-cities-instead-of-smart-ones)\n\t- [The rise of the dumb city](https://citymonitor.ai/environment/architecture-design/smart-dumb-city)\n\t- [TreesAI](https://treesasinfrastructure.com/)\n### Localization\nEvery bioregion has a slightly different metabolism, determined by its unique combination of climate, geology, ecology, and culture. Our production processes and social systems need to be adapted to these local contexts, incorporating infrastructure into that unique metabolism. This approach fosters a deeper connection between production and place, encouraging the development of locally-adapted solutions and the sharing of knowledge and resources within bioregional networks.\n### Incorporating Limits\nIncorporating limits is about both respecting the boundaries of ecosystems and designing technologies that embody restraint. In nature, limits are not constraints but rather the very conditions that enable creativity and resilience. By incorporating limits into the design of our technologies, we can foster innovation and adaptability. This approach, exemplified by the Low Tech movement, treats limits as design constraints that stimulate creative problem-solving and inclusive solutions.\n## Radical Care","x":14472,"y":-414,"width":720,"height":1860},
		{"id":"892b9b9cc8a4a2b1","type":"text","text":"This includes elements like laws, family systems, educational institutions, and economic hierarchies, which together create a framework that governs social order and expectations. ","x":14112,"y":-64,"width":300,"height":241},
		{"id":"09512fb56a5ff131","type":"text","text":"## How does it interact with technology?\nAs technology advances, it influences our collective consciousness, altering perceptions, values, and ideologies. For example, the rise of social media has revolutionized how we see the world, shaping our beliefs about community, identity, and even truth itself. These technological shifts permeate our superstructure, transforming not just how we connect and communicate, but also how we conceptualize reality and our place within it. \n\n## How does it interact with technology?\nTechnology enables new values and ways of interacting, which challenge existing social structures, prompting adaptations in laws, economic systems, and social norms to accommodate and regulate these changes, thereby continually reshaping the framework of society.\n\n## How does it interact with technology?\nTechnologies exist within ecologies, groups of technologies that are symbiotically related and co-evolve together. For instance, a light bulb, lamp, power lines, transformers, and power station form an ecology of technologies. Multiple different ecologies of technology embedded together to form a basic part of social coordination and material reproduction within a society, aka infrastructure. ","x":13992,"y":236,"width":420,"height":1210},
		{"id":"83c0cefd30ee471a","type":"file","file":"5 🌰 Seeds/1.3.1 Superstructure.md","x":10860,"y":-804,"width":580,"height":2080},
		{"id":"675835f9a3226d37","type":"text","text":"> _Technologies are not mere exterior aids, but also interior transformations of consciousness, and never more than when they affect the world._ [Walter Ong](https://www.jstor.org/stable/43501355).","x":10900,"y":1116,"width":400,"height":120},
		{"id":"a6ceaba6372adf6d","type":"file","file":"5 🌰 Seeds/1.3.2 Social Structure.md","x":11600,"y":-804,"width":580,"height":2080},
		{"id":"cb6d7f7c8e5c9a33","type":"file","file":"5 🌰 Seeds/1.3.3 Infrastructure.md","x":12320,"y":-804,"width":580,"height":2080},
		{"id":"ae3df1d081e3e326","type":"file","file":"1 🌳 Trunks/1.3 Infrastructure.md","x":10080,"y":-804,"width":720,"height":2860},
		{"id":"2a4a5a4dbb9d7cf0","type":"text","text":"As naturalist John Muir famously observed, \"when we try to pick out anything by itself, we find it _hitched to everything else in the Universe_.\" When we examine any single piece of technology, we find it inextricably linked to a vast web of interconnections.\n\nThe internet has made us acutely aware of technological interconnectedness, but this web of relationships extends far beyond digital networks. Consider a single computer: it's not just a standalone device, but a nexus of global networks. Its components are sourced through international supply chains, assembled using knowledge shared across continents, powered by electricity flowing through vast grids, and operated with software developed by distributed teams ==(all managed/connected through money)==.\n\nEven a seemingly simple object like a woven basket reveals similar complexity: it embodies information passed down through generations via language, consists of materials like wood that are part of vast ecological networks, requires human energy sustained by intricate food systems, and relies on social structures that allow for specialization. An accurate definition of  technology must account for all of these elements coming together through intricate systems.","x":-4252,"y":2584,"width":600,"height":560},
		{"id":"b64e9654fd326c9d","type":"text","text":"**Networks**: The structural frameworks that facilitate interactions between energy, materials, and information. ","x":-4252,"y":3164,"width":250,"height":160},
		{"id":"5f015c2a0622aedf","type":"text","text":"Introduce core ideas in the essay: To understand how we can build tech that helps us thrive within planetary boundaries successfully, we need to understand what tech is, and how it impacts how we live together on this planet","x":9440,"y":-564,"width":250,"height":300,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"21a9513598fbe412","type":"file","file":"0 🌞 About/0.0 Technology for a Regenerative Society.md","x":8720,"y":-804,"width":700,"height":1140},
		{"id":"7e573b38af8c5ba5","type":"text","text":"#### Exponential technology\nExponential technologies are those capable of self-replication or compounding growth, allowing them to multiply their impacts – for better or worse – at an accelerating rate. Agriculture was the original exponential technology, enabling human populations to expand rapidly while disconnecting us from being an integrated part of natural ecosystems. \n\nThe development of nuclear weapons marked a pivotal moment when humanity acquired an exponential technology with the potential to trigger global catastrophe. Today, emerging technologies like artificial intelligence, biotechnology, and advanced manufacturing techniques are democratizing the ability to wield world-altering forces of creation and destruction. Unless we establish new frameworks for responsibly harnessing such exponential technologies, we risk irreparable damage to our world.\n#### Externalities\nExternalities are the unaccounted-for consequences of our actions. They arise from a linear, reductionist mindset that fails to account for the intricate web of interconnections and feedback loops operating across social, economic, and environmental domains.\n\nOur current civilizational model is predicated on the perpetual growth of consumption and extraction, generating a torrent of externalities that are undermining the very ecological foundations of human prosperity. From climate change and biodiversity loss to toxic pollution and the degradation of natural resources, externalities are rapidly destabilizing the Earth's regulatory systems.\n\nMoreover, our technological systems are producing pernicious social and psychological externalities, from the erosion of privacy and autonomy to the fragmentation of public discourse and the proliferation of mental health issues exacerbated by social media and digital addictions.\n\nAs these externalities compound and propagate through complex systemic feedbacks, they are accelerating the degradation of our ecological and social capital – the integrated totality of life-sustaining resources and socioeconomic resilience upon which all civilizations ultimately depend.\n\n![[Ecocultural Capital.png]]\n#### Multi-polar traps\nMulti-polar traps are interconnected dilemmas where the rational choices for individual actors directly undermine the collective good. These traps arise when the pursuit of narrow self-interest generates negative externalities that ultimately harm all parties involved.\n\nClassic examples include the tragedy of the commons, where individuals deplete shared resources for personal gain, and regulatory races-to-the-bottom, where jurisdictions compete to attract business by lowering environmental and labor standards. In our globalized world, multi-polar traps are becoming increasingly pervasive and severe. Nations prioritize economic competitiveness over environmental sustainability, while corporations off-shore pollution and exploit regulatory havens.\n\nAs the interconnectedness and complexity of our civilizational systems intensify, these multi-polar traps are becoming more acute and intractable, frustrating our ability to address collective challenges like climate change, resource depletion, and economic inequality.\n### Transition Pathways\nHumanity now faces three broad potential trajectories or \"attractors\" as we grapple with these civilizational crises:\n\n1. **Techno-Authoritarianism**: Where technological acceleration centralizes power and resources in the hands of a small elite, leading to extreme inequality, social unrest, and authoritarian control.\n2. **Civilizational Collapse**: Where compounding crises and cascading systems failures trigger a descent into chaos, conflict, and the unraveling of complex human societies.\n3. **[[1.3 Infrastructure]]**: Where humanity transcends its current dysfunctional paradigms through the cooperative creation of new distributed and decentralized models for organizing technology, energy, and resources to cultivate a thriving future for all.\n\nRealizing the emancipatory potential of a [[1.3 Infrastructure]] will require nothing less than a profound civilizational transition. This transition is already underway, catalyzed by the democratizing forces of internet technologies and the imperative to harness new, renewable forms of energy to power a new format of human civilization.\n\nYet the path ahead remains uncertain, fraught with obstacles and requiring unprecedented levels of cooperation, foresight, and innovation across all sectors of human endeavor. \n\nOnly by reconstructing our systems from first principles can we navigate the turbulent transitions ahead and give birth to an anti-fragile, [[1.3 Infrastructure]] capable of flourishing through these challenger.","x":-4900,"y":7115,"width":642,"height":1194},
		{"id":"ee70ee8606ad3bad","type":"text","text":"By structuring information, energy and materials through networks, humans turn shared purpose into a large cybernetic system we call civilization. A cybernetic system is one that continuously responds to internal and external forces by creating loops between learning and action. Cybernetic systems can be biological, like our bodies; technological, like a thermostat; institutional, like a corporation; or a mixture of all three, like civilization.","x":-4440,"y":6753,"width":250,"height":355},
		{"id":"5687d82ac09bf761","type":"text","text":"Something about how tech helps us adapt and thrive in our environment. How tech is created by reacting to external forces: ecosystem pressures, like the coconut. Also internal forces: other tech like social institutions, or money","x":-4400,"y":6415,"width":250,"height":300},
		{"id":"0cbebb16fa1ff7ea","type":"text","text":"Discuss IEMM theory without fully committing to it, also talk about how superstructure changes might come first","x":-3742,"y":7733,"width":250,"height":179,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3da6c7d0b9f31cbd","type":"text","text":"Link article here about money being the transition","x":-3742,"y":7914,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"1293fd60385d0737","type":"text","text":"There's an argument that subjugation is what allowed for both major transitions > paying tribute & colonization","x":-3742,"y":7991,"width":250,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"7b3d522db47000b1","type":"text","text":"1. civilisystems in history & how they have been shaped by the different elements\n2. Theories around how these changes happen, how all the elements affect each other","x":-3492,"y":7454,"width":250,"height":279,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"d3fe01e714b7837e","type":"text","text":"### Metasystems in Civilization\nCivilizational metasystems emerge when structural changes exert pressures that demand greater information processing capabilities from a society. The emergence of new technologies for sharing and processing information - like the development of language, writing, or the printing press - enables qualitatively new forms of interaction between human groups. These novel interaction potentials then pave the way for higher levels of societal integration and more complex organizational structures to take shape.","x":-3454,"y":7806,"width":425,"height":370},
		{"id":"ed0a4a71fa2d5900","type":"text","text":"Before, civilizations could be considered different cybernetic entities because they didn't share pretty much any of the same systems. Now there is just 1.","x":-3241,"y":7559,"width":250,"height":200,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"a427ce9a43dbc809","type":"text","text":"The inability to cooperate effectively as human groups for the betterment of all","x":-3820,"y":8443,"width":250,"height":140,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"3f5fa5c8054f6900","type":"text","text":"Not integrating consequences into our systems: bad view of reality, not seeing the impact of our actions","x":-3820,"y":8583,"width":250,"height":160,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4b333ff6c06f97c6","type":"text","text":"Expo tech: The capacity to have enormous (& rapidly increasing) impact on the world","x":-3820,"y":8743,"width":250,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"31949eb51edca2a5","type":"text","text":"Explain Metasystem Transition?","x":-3454,"y":8173,"width":250,"height":90,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4742ec69fc7d498f","type":"text","text":"Incorporate info control theory around how these civilizations worked?","x":-3742,"y":8203,"width":250,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"4eb830b5c3be3736","type":"text","text":"Use the example of iriqois confederacy to show how new metasystems require new structures ie the peace thing","x":-3454,"y":8263,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"e1b9aa467d98319c","type":"text","text":"This is about how technology shapes civilization","x":-3800,"y":7454,"width":250,"height":105,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"b8d0757ce5ea5e9c","type":"text","text":"Different theories about what is most important","x":-3799,"y":7394,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"000aad1d541f3449","type":"text","text":"How does technological advancement allow for new metasystems and new kinds of metabolisms?","x":-3799,"y":7559,"width":250,"height":117,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"45454c6573af8dcb","type":"text","text":"Bring in technology more clearly in all of these examples","x":900,"y":5540,"width":250,"height":120,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"8ac7aebe937acf52","type":"text","text":"How tech influences & is influenced by each one of these","x":900,"y":5660,"width":250,"height":100,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"9b73d89f4d980b63","type":"text","text":"Social structure is tech, infra is tech, super is not tech but produced by. What about meditation techniques? Where do they sit?","x":900,"y":5760,"width":250,"height":180,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"5e74daa7ad0696a8","x":-1420,"y":5540,"width":540,"height":600,"type":"text","text":"#### The Ethical Implications of Technological Agency\nRecognizing the agency of technology has profound ethical implications. It suggests that the development and deployment of new technologies is not a neutral act, but one that actively shapes the future of human society and our relationship with the world around us.\n\nThis understanding calls for a more thoughtful and intentional approach to technological development. We must consider not just the immediate utility of a technology, but its potential to reshape our world in ways we might not initially anticipate.\n\nMoreover, it highlights the importance of diversity in technological development. Different cultures and perspectives may develop technologies that embody different values and ways of interacting with the world. A monoculture of technology risks constraining human potential and limiting our collective ability to address complex global challenges.\n\nIn conclusion, technologies are not merely tools we use, but active agents that shape our reality. They create new possibilities and constraints, altering how we perceive and interact with the world. As we continue to develop new technologies, we must remain mindful of their transformative power and strive to harness this power in ways that align with our broader goals for human flourishing and planetary well-being."},
		{"id":"08531f87c75265d5","type":"text","text":"The interaction of purpose with internal and external forces","x":-1435,"y":1360,"width":300,"height":60,"color":"5"},
		{"id":"54dd8a609d8982b8","x":-1565,"y":2369,"width":325,"height":278,"type":"text","text":"\nFor example, instead of evolving thick fur to survive in cold climates, we invented clothing and heating systems. Rather than developing gills to explore underwater ecosystems, we created diving equipment and submarines. This technological adaptation is much faster and more flexible than biological evolution, allowing us to thrive in a wide range of ecosystems."},
		{"id":"a0036711e96d36c3","type":"text","text":"It comprises the physical tools, techniques, and systems that we create to meet our needs and shape our interactions with our ecosystems. ","x":-1891,"y":-685,"width":250,"height":185},
		{"id":"06a4eb20a6f595aa","x":440,"y":-1160,"width":740,"height":8860,"type":"file","file":"Do Not Publish/Institutions & Civilization.md"},
		{"id":"d2e1659244b809c3","x":-1662,"y":2762,"width":520,"height":782,"type":"text","text":"These constraints and affordances operate across multiple domains:\n1. Physical Realm:\n   In the physical world, technologies alter our relationship with materials, energy, and space.\n   Example: Consider a simple rock used as a tool to crack open a coconut. This technology affords us the ability to access the coconut's interior, which was previously constrained by our bare hands. However, it also introduces new constraints - the need to find suitable rocks and the risk of injury if used improperly.\n\n2. Realm of Relationships:\n   Technologies can fundamentally alter the nature and scope of human relationships.\n   Example: The institution of marriage, viewed as a social technology, binds people together for child-rearing and resource sharing. It affords stability and social recognition but also constrains personal freedom in various ways. Digital communication technologies have further reshaped relationships, affording instant global communication but potentially constraining deep, in-person connections.\n\n3. Realm of Values:\n   Technologies can shape our values, beliefs, and ethical frameworks.\n   Example: The invention of the clock altered our perception of time, leading to values of punctuality and efficiency that were less prominent in pre-clock societies. Similarly, social media technologies have reshaped our notions of privacy, self-expression, and social validation."},
		{"id":"c922d299bcd0558d","x":1240,"y":4360,"width":360,"height":280,"type":"text","text":"### Cybernetic civilization\n==(Not only biological organisims can be cybernetic, also tech or organizational. You might have heard the term cyborg, usually used to describe something like terminator... joke about cyborgs here, civilization is a cyborg)==\n\n\n\n==(the 2 examples here should start with a more detailed explanation of the local ecosystem & multi-species relationships, before diving into the 3 structures. Also show from a cybernetic perspective how each would adapt to a challenge)==\nExample: Ancient Egyptian Civilization\nAncient Egypt evolved around the Nile River ecosystem, demonstrating how a civilization adapts to and shapes its environment:\n- Superstructure: Egyptian religion centered around the Nile's annual flood cycle, with gods like Hapi representing the flood. This belief system provided meaning to natural phenomena and guided societal actions.\n- Social Structure: A hierarchical society developed, with the Pharaoh at the top, believed to control the Nile's floods through divine power. This structure organized labor for agriculture and massive construction projects.\n- Infrastructure: Egyptians developed sophisticated irrigation systems, flood control mechanisms, and agricultural practices tailored to the Nile's cycle.\n\nExample: Mesopotamian Civilization\nIn contrast, Mesopotamian civilizations developed in a less predictable river valley environment:\n- Superstructure: Mesopotamian religions often portrayed nature as chaotic and potentially hostile, reflecting the unpredictable floods of the Tigris and Euphrates.\n- Social Structure: City-states emerged, each with its own ruler and patron deity, reflecting the more fragmented landscape.\n- Infrastructure: Extensive canal systems and levees were built to control flooding and distribute water, showing adaptation to the challenging environment.\n\nThese examples illustrate how civilizations, as cybernetic systems, continuously adapt to their environments. The interplay between superstructure, social structure, and infrastructure shapes these adaptations, leading to diverse outcomes even in similar situations. ==(for example, lets look at how different superstructures affected adaptation==)\n\n==(edit examples bellow to flow better, more like stories)==\nDifferent Superstructures, Different Adaptations:\n1. Ancestral Puebloans vs. Aztecs in face of drought:\n- Ancestral Puebloans:\n    - Superstructure: Believed in harmony with nature\n    - Adaptation: Developed sophisticated water harvesting techniques, eventually migrating when these proved insufficient\n- Aztecs:\n    - Superstructure: Believed in the need to nourish gods through sacrifice\n    - Adaptation: Intensified human sacrifice during droughts, developed chinampas (floating gardens) for agriculture\n2. Industrial Revolution in Britain vs. Japan:\n- Britain:\n    - Superstructure: Protestant work ethic, belief in progress through science\n    - Adaptation: Rapid industrialization, urbanization, and colonial expansion\n- Japan (Meiji Era):\n    - Superstructure: Confucian values, emphasis on national strength\n    - Adaptation: Selective modernization, preserving traditional social structures while adopting Western technology\n\n==(conclusion paragraph here)=="},
		{"id":"91dffbc792dd65c8","type":"text","text":"Not exactly right","x":1100,"y":1600,"width":250,"height":60,"color":"3"},
		{"id":"1430c76fdb657592","type":"text","text":"### Purpose\nIn the context of technology, purpose refers to the intended goal or outcome that the technology is designed to achieve. In natural systems, the purpose of a technology is often related to the survival and flourishing of the organism or ecosystem. For example, a bird's nest is a technology that serves the purpose of protecting eggs and young chicks, ultimately contributing to the survival and reproduction of the species.\n\nHowever, in human systems, the notion of purpose can be more complex and multifaceted. While many technologies are indeed designed to enhance human survival and well-being, others may serve more abstract or subjective goals, such as entertainment, artistic expression, or the exercise of power and control.\n\nMoreover, the purpose of a technology can have unintended consequences or serve the interests of some stakeholders at the expense of others. For instance, social media platforms may aim to connect people and facilitate communication, but they can also contribute to the spread of misinformation, the erosion of privacy, and the polarization of society.\n\nIt's crucial to recognize that the purpose of a technology is not always neutral or universally benign. The development and deployment of technologies often reflect the values, biases, and power dynamics of the individuals and institutions involved. A technology designed to enhance the efficiency of a manufacturing process may prioritize profit margins over worker well-being or environmental sustainability.\n\nTherefore, when considering the purpose of a technology, it's essential to ask: Who or what is meant to benefit from this technology? What are the potential unintended consequences or trade-offs involved? Whose interests and values are being prioritized, and who may be disadvantaged or excluded?\n\nBy critically examining the purpose of a technology, we can develop a more nuanced understanding of its social, ethical, and political implications. This understanding can help us make more informed decisions about the development, governance, and use of technologies in ways that align with our shared values and goals as a society.\n\nIn summary, the purpose of a technology refers to the intended goal or outcome it is designed to achieve. While technologies can serve to enhance human survival and flourishing, their purposes can also be complex, multifaceted, and potentially controversial. By critically examining the purpose of a technology, we can better understand its broader implications and make more informed decisions about its development and use.","x":-1540,"y":1740,"width":720,"height":522}
	],
	"edges":[]
}